# Activity 03 - CSS Stylesheet Methods

**Course:** CS319 - Construction of User Interfaces
**Author:** Evan Litzer (bubaq@iastate.edu)
**Date:** September 4-5, 2025

## Overview

This activity demonstrates three different methods of applying CSS styles to HTML documents:
- Inline CSS
- Internal (Embedded) CSS
- External CSS

## Project Structure

```
Activity03/
├── Demo (PDF)/
│   ├── bubaq_Activity03_inline_CSS.html       # Inline styling example
│   ├── bubaq_Activity03_internal_CSS.html     # Internal stylesheet example
│   ├── bubaq_Activity03_external_CSS.html     # External stylesheet example
│   ├── bubaq_Activity03_external.css          # External CSS file
│   ├── bubaq_Activity03_challenge.html        # Combined styling challenge
│   └── bubaq_Activity03_challenge.css         # Challenge external styles
└── Superhero (HTML)/
    └── bubaq_Activity03_superhero_challenge.html  # Advanced CSS challenge
```

## Files Description

### Demo Files

1. **Inline CSS** (`bubaq_Activity03_inline_CSS.html`)
   - Demonstrates styling directly within HTML elements using the `style` attribute
   - Simple examples with colored headings and paragraphs

2. **Internal CSS** (`bubaq_Activity03_internal_CSS.html`)
   - Shows CSS rules defined within `<style>` tags in the document head
   - Combines external and internal stylesheet references

3. **External CSS** (`bubaq_Activity03_external_CSS.html`)
   - Links to an external stylesheet file
   - Demonstrates CSS class selectors (`.myh1class`, `.mypclass`)

4. **Challenge Demo** (`bubaq_Activity03_challenge.html`)
   - Combines all three CSS methods in a single document
   - Shows external styles for header section
   - Internal styles for "About Us" section
   - Inline styles for "Contact Us" section

### Superhero Challenge

**Superhero Profiles** (`bubaq_Activity03_superhero_challenge.html`)
- Advanced CSS styling exercise featuring superhero profiles
- Demonstrates:
  - Element selectors (`h1`, `h2`, `p`)
  - Class selectors (`.abilities`, `.bio`, `.superhero-title`, `.details-btn`)
  - ID selectors (`#wonder-woman`, `#spider-man`)
  - Pseudo-class selectors (`:hover`)
  - Various CSS properties: colors, borders, shadows, padding, fonts, and button styling

## Key Concepts Covered

- **CSS Specificity**: Understanding how inline > internal > external styles are applied
- **CSS Selectors**: Element, class, and ID selectors
- **Box Model**: Padding, borders, and margins
- **Typography**: Font families, sizes, weights, and text styling
- **Colors**: Hex codes, RGB values, and named colors
- **Interactive States**: Hover effects and cursor changes
- **Layout**: Text alignment and centering

## How to Run

1. Open any `.html` file in a web browser
2. For external CSS examples, ensure the corresponding `.css` files are in the same directory
3. Interact with elements (hover over headings, buttons) to see dynamic styles

## Learning Objectives

- Understand the three methods of applying CSS to HTML
- Practice using different CSS selector types
- Learn CSS specificity and cascade rules
- Apply common CSS properties for text, colors, and layout
